function curriculumChunk6_() {
  return {"grade6":{"g6-main-w7-core":{"id":"g6-main-w7-core","number":13,"week":7,"track":"main","type":"core","title":"Signature Solution - Planning","summary":"Students plan a five-part signature solution - goal, prompt structure, inputs, evaluation method and risk plan - and take it through an approve-to-build gate.","duration":"45 minutes","strands":["CU","SD","CE","GE"],"objectives":["Plan a structured AI solution: state the goal, the prompt structure, and the check steps.","Identify the inputs/data and the output-evaluation method.","Describe one risk and how the plan reduces it."],"sections":[{"type":"text","body":["Today you become a designer, not just a prompter. Before anyone types a single word into an AI tool, you will plan a small solution with five parts that fit together: a goal, a prompt structure, inputs, a check, and a risk plan. A good plan is like a bridge design drawn before the first bolt is placed - it lets you find weak spots on paper, where fixing them is cheap."],"role":"intro"},{"type":"list","heading":"I can…","items":["I can state the goal of my solution in one clear sentence.","I can lay out a prompt structure with a role, a task, and one rule.","I can name the inputs my solution needs and how I will check the output.","I can describe one risk and one way my plan reduces it."],"role":"objectives"},{"type":"vocabulary","heading":"Words to know","terms":[{"term":"Signature solution","definition":"A small, reusable AI design with a clear goal and connected parts that solves one simple task."},{"term":"Prompt structure","definition":"The shape of a prompt: a role for the AI, a clear task, and at least one rule or limit."},{"term":"Input","definition":"The information a prompt works on, chosen to contain no personal or identifying data."},{"term":"Evaluation method","definition":"The agreed way you will judge whether an output is accurate."},{"term":"Risk mitigation","definition":"A design choice that lowers the chance or impact of something going wrong."}],"role":"vocabulary"},{"type":"text","heading":"From one prompt to a whole solution","body":["In Grade 5 you improved a single prompt. This term you design a solution: a set of parts that work together to do one simple job well.","The five parts are the goal, the [[prompt structure]], the inputs, the [[evaluation method]], and the risk plan. If any part is missing, the solution is harder to trust."],"role":"explain"},{"type":"text","heading":"Writing a prompt structure","body":["A [[prompt structure]] names a role ('You are a timetable helper'), a task ('list the three earliest buses'), and one rule ('if a time is missing, say so').","The role focuses the AI, the task says exactly what to do, and the rule keeps the output honest and easy to check."],"role":"explain"},{"type":"text","heading":"Checks and risks before you build","body":["An [[evaluation method]] says how you will judge accuracy, such as comparing the output to a known-correct answer key.","A risk plan names one thing that could go wrong, like a confident wrong answer, and one design choice that reduces it, like a 'say if unsure' rule plus a human check. Never put personal or identifying data into a prompt."],"role":"explain"},{"type":"activity","label":"Hands-on","heading":"Let's try it - hands-on","body":["You will now plan your own signature solution on the plan sheet. You will not log in to any tool; the teacher runs any AI on the board. Your job is to make the plan complete and safe."],"items":["Read your task card and write the goal in one clear sentence.","Fill the role-task-rule slots for your prompt structure.","List the inputs your prompt needs, checking there is no personal data.","Write your evaluation method and one risk with its mitigation.","Self-check against the five-part list, then submit for the approve-to-build gate."],"role":"activity"},{"type":"list","heading":"Think about it","items":["Which part of a plan is easiest to forget, and why does that matter?","How does a 'say if unsure' rule make an output safer to trust?"],"role":"discuss"},{"type":"list","heading":"My reflection","items":["Write the one part of your plan you are most confident about.","Write the one part you will strengthen before building next week."],"role":"reflect"}],"worksheet":{"title":"Week 7 - Core - Worksheet | Signature Solution - Planning","questions":[{"id":"g6-main-w7-core-aq1","type":"mcq","marks":2,"prompt":"Layla planned a lost-property helper. Goal: \"Sort the found-items list into categories so items get back to their owners faster.\" Prompt structure: \"You are a lost-property helper. Sort each item into clothing, devices or other. If an item does not fit a category, label it other.\" Inputs: \"a made-up list of ten found items, with no names on it.\" Risk: \"the AI might put an item in the wrong category, so a person reads the sorted list before it is posted.\" Which one of the five plan parts is still missing?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The evaluation method - she has not said how she will judge whether the sorting is accurate."},{"id":"b","text":"The goal - her first sentence does not say what the solution is for."},{"id":"c","text":"The inputs - she has not said what information the prompt works on."},{"id":"d","text":"The risk mitigation - she names a risk but does nothing about it."}],"answer":"a","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}],"evidenceType":"product","feedback":{"correct":"Right - Layla has a risk plan but no agreed way to judge accuracy, such as checking the sorted list against a teacher's answer key.","incorrect":"Look again: the goal, the inputs and a mitigation (a person checks before posting) are all there. Nothing tells you how she will judge whether the sorting is accurate."}},{"id":"g6-main-w7-core-aq2","type":"matching","marks":4,"prompt":"Omar wrote these five lines on his plan sheet for a bus-timetable helper. Match each line to the part of the plan it fills.","left":[{"id":"l1","text":"\"Help new students find the three earliest buses home after clubs.\""},{"id":"l2","text":"\"You are a timetable helper. List the three earliest buses. If a time is missing, say so.\""},{"id":"l3","text":"\"A made-up timetable of twelve bus times, with no student names on it.\""},{"id":"l4","text":"\"I will compare the three times the AI lists against the teacher's answer key.\""},{"id":"l5","text":"\"The AI might invent a bus time, so a person checks the three times before they go on the noticeboard.\""}],"right":[{"id":"r1","text":"The goal"},{"id":"r2","text":"The prompt structure"},{"id":"r3","text":"The inputs"},{"id":"r4","text":"The evaluation method"},{"id":"r5","text":"The risk mitigation"}],"answer":{"l1":"r1","l2":"r2","l3":"r3","l4":"r4","l5":"r5"},"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}],"evidenceType":"product","feedback":{"correct":"Well matched - a plan is complete when all five parts are written down and each one does its own job.","incorrect":"Check the last two: the evaluation method says how you judge accuracy, while the risk mitigation names something that could go wrong and the choice that reduces it."}},{"id":"g6-main-w7-core-aq3","type":"truefalse","marks":1,"prompt":"Sara says: \"My risk plan already says a person will check the output, so I do not need an evaluation method as well.\" Is Sara right?","answer":false,"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}],"evidenceType":"product","feedback":{"correct":"Correct - the evaluation method tells that person what to compare the output against, so without it the check has no standard to judge by.","incorrect":"The two parts do different jobs: the evaluation method sets how accuracy is judged (for example, against an answer key), and the risk plan lowers the chance or impact of a failure."}},{"id":"g6-main-w7-core-aq4","type":"ordering","marks":4,"prompt":"Put the steps of the plan-sheet routine into the order the lesson gives them.","items":[{"id":"s1","text":"Read the task card and write the goal in one clear sentence."},{"id":"s2","text":"Fill the role, task and rule slots of the prompt structure."},{"id":"s3","text":"List the inputs the prompt needs, checking there is no personal data."},{"id":"s4","text":"Write the evaluation method and one risk with its mitigation."},{"id":"s5","text":"Self-check against the five-part list, then submit for the approve-to-build gate."}],"answer":["s1","s2","s3","s4","s5"],"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}],"evidenceType":"product","feedback":{"correct":"Exactly - the goal comes first because every later part has to serve it, and the gate comes last because it checks a finished plan.","incorrect":"The goal is written first, the prompt structure and inputs are built to serve it, the check and risk come next, and the approve-to-build gate is the last step."}},{"id":"g6-main-w7-core-aq5","type":"multi","marks":3,"prompt":"Rashid's solution sorts a found-items list. The risk he names is: \"the AI might add an item that was never on my list, and sound sure about it.\" Choose the TWO design choices in his plan that would reduce that risk.","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Add the rule: \"Use only the items on the list I give you. If you are unsure about an item, say so.\""},{"id":"b","text":"Check the sorted list against the original found-items list before anything is posted."},{"id":"c","text":"Make the goal sentence longer so the AI is given more detail to work with."},{"id":"d","text":"Give the AI twenty items at a time instead of ten so it has more to compare."},{"id":"e","text":"Ask the AI to sound more confident so the sorted list is easier to read."}],"answer":["a","b"],"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}],"evidenceType":"product","feedback":{"correct":"Yes - a rule that limits the AI to the given items plus a human check against the original list both make an invented item far less likely to slip through.","incorrect":"A longer goal, a bigger batch or a more confident tone do not stop an invented item. What stops it is a rule that ties the AI to your list and a check against that list."}}]},"formative":{"targets":"Checks planning of the signature solution against the Emerging project criteria","check":"Plan review against the project rubric (Emerging); approve-to-build gate","successCriteria":"Plan states goal, prompt structure, inputs, an evaluation method, and one risk mitigation.","evidence":"Approved plan sheet."}},"g6-main-w7-lab":{"id":"g6-main-w7-lab","number":14,"week":7,"track":"main","type":"lab","title":"Prototype and Pressure-Test the Plan (Lab)","summary":"Students prototype their approved plan on the board, break it on purpose with a tricky curated input, and design an if-then fallback branch that keeps a person in the loop.","duration":"45 minutes","strands":["CU","SD","CE","GE"],"objectives":["Prototype the prompt structure and pressure-test the plan against a tricky input.","Add a fallback branch for when the output fails a check.","Record what changed and why after the pressure test."],"sections":[{"type":"text","body":["A plan on paper looks tidy. Today you find out how it behaves under pressure. You will build a quick prototype, then throw your hardest inputs at it on purpose - the missing item, the messy note, the two-in-one line. When it stumbles, you will not hide the failure; you will design a fallback branch that catches it safely. Engineers stress-test bridges before people cross them. You will stress-test your solution before it is trusted."],"role":"intro"},{"type":"list","heading":"I can…","items":["I can run my prompt structure on a normal input and read the output.","I can pressure-test it with a tricky input and spot where it fails.","I can add a fallback branch that says what to do when a check fails.","I can record the change I made and the reason for it."],"role":"objectives"},{"type":"vocabulary","heading":"Words to know","terms":[{"term":"Prototype","definition":"A first working version built quickly to see how a design really behaves."},{"term":"Pressure test","definition":"Deliberately trying a tricky input to reveal a weak spot."},{"term":"Edge case","definition":"An unusual input at the edge of what a solution expects, like a missing or double value."},{"term":"Fallback branch","definition":"A planned 'if a check fails, then...' response that handles a failure safely."},{"term":"Error type","definition":"A label for what went wrong: wrong, unclear, or missing."}],"role":"vocabulary"},{"type":"text","heading":"Prototype first, judge second","body":["A [[prototype]] is a fast first version. Running it shows how your design behaves, not just how you hoped it would.","The teacher runs your prompt on the board with a normal curated input. You read the output and decide: did it meet the goal?"],"role":"explain"},{"type":"text","heading":"Design the fallback branch","body":["When a check fails, a good solution follows a [[fallback branch]] instead of giving a confident wrong answer.","Write it as 'If the check fails, then ___ and ask a person.' Keeping a human in the loop is part of responsible design.","Remember: only curated, made-up inputs - never real names or personal data."],"role":"explain"},{"type":"activity","label":"Hands-on","heading":"Let's try it - hands-on","body":["You will prototype your approved plan, pressure-test it, and add a fallback branch. The teacher runs the AI on the board; you do not log in. Log every run."],"items":["Copy your prompt structure into the prototype slot of your log.","Watch the normal-input run on the board and record whether it met the goal.","Pressure-test with a tricky curated input and record the error type.","Write your fallback branch as an if-then sentence that asks a person.","Log the one change you made and the reason it will help."],"role":"activity"},{"type":"list","heading":"Think about it","items":["Which tricky input broke the most prototypes, and why?","Why is a handled failure safer than a confident wrong answer?"],"role":"discuss"},{"type":"list","heading":"My reflection","items":["Write the weak spot your pressure test exposed.","Write how your fallback branch will handle it next week."],"role":"reflect"}],"worksheet":{"title":"Week 7 - Lab - Worksheet | Prototype and Pressure-Test the Plan (Lab)","questions":[{"id":"g6-main-w7-lab-aq1","type":"mcq","marks":2,"prompt":"Bilal's prototype has the task \"label each found item as clothing, devices or other\". For his pressure test he uses the tricky note \"found: a charger and a scarf\". The output reads: \"devices\". Using the lesson's three error-type labels, which error type should he log?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Missing - the note held two items and one of them has come back with no label."},{"id":"b","text":"Wrong - the charger has been put into the incorrect category."},{"id":"c","text":"Unclear - the word \"devices\" could mean more than one thing."},{"id":"d","text":"No error - the prompt asked for a label, and one label came back."}],"answer":"a","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}],"evidenceType":"product","feedback":{"correct":"Right - the charger is labelled correctly, so nothing is wrong; what the two-in-one note exposed is a whole item being dropped.","incorrect":"The label \"devices\" fits the charger, so this is not a wrong answer - the weak spot is the scarf, which never got a label at all."}},{"id":"g6-main-w7-lab-aq2","type":"multi","marks":3,"prompt":"Bilal now writes up that failure in his build/test log. Choose the TWO entries that meet what the lesson asks for: a fallback branch and a logged reason.","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Fallback: \"If a note names more than one item, then label each item on its own line, and if one still does not fit, label it other and ask a person.\""},{"id":"b","text":"Reason: \"My pressure test showed a two-in-one note loses an item, so splitting the note before labelling stops items being dropped.\""},{"id":"c","text":"Fallback: \"If the output looks wrong, then run the prompt again until it looks right.\""},{"id":"d","text":"Fallback: \"If a note names more than one item, then label the first item and move on.\""},{"id":"e","text":"Reason: \"I changed my prompt because I wanted the output to sound better.\""}],"answer":["a","b"],"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}],"evidenceType":"product","feedback":{"correct":"Yes - the branch is an if-then that handles the failure he actually saw and keeps a person in the loop, and the reason points back to what the run showed.","incorrect":"Re-running until it looks right, or labelling only the first item, still loses the scarf, and \"I wanted it to sound better\" is not a reason drawn from the test."}},{"id":"g6-main-w7-lab-aq3","type":"truefalse","marks":1,"prompt":"Maya says: \"A prototype that always gives a confident answer, even on a messy input, is safer than one that stops and asks a person.\" Is Maya right?","answer":false,"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}],"evidenceType":"product","feedback":{"correct":"Correct - a confident wrong answer gets used without anyone noticing, while a handled failure sends the messy case to a person who can sort it out.","incorrect":"Think about who catches the mistake: a confident answer on a messy input hides the failure, but a fallback branch that asks a person makes it visible and safe."}},{"id":"g6-main-w7-lab-aq4","type":"ordering","marks":4,"prompt":"Put the steps of the lab routine into the order the lesson gives them.","items":[{"id":"s1","text":"Copy the approved prompt structure into the prototype slot of the log."},{"id":"s2","text":"Watch the normal-input run on the board and record whether it met the goal."},{"id":"s3","text":"Pressure-test with a tricky curated input and record the error type."},{"id":"s4","text":"Write the fallback branch as an if-then sentence that asks a person."},{"id":"s5","text":"Log the one change made and the reason it will help."}],"answer":["s1","s2","s3","s4","s5"],"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}],"evidenceType":"product","feedback":{"correct":"Exactly - the normal run comes before the pressure test so you know the prototype works at all, and the branch is written only once a real weak spot has shown itself.","incorrect":"You cannot design a fallback for a failure you have not seen yet: prototype, run it normally, pressure-test it, then write the branch and log the reason."}},{"id":"g6-main-w7-lab-aq5","type":"matching","marks":4,"prompt":"Four groups pressure-tested the same found-items prototype and each exposed a different weak spot. Match each weak spot to the fallback branch that handles it.","left":[{"id":"l1","text":"The note held two items, but only one came back with a label."},{"id":"l2","text":"A scarf came back labelled \"devices\"."},{"id":"l3","text":"The output said \"maybe clothing, or possibly other\"."},{"id":"l4","text":"The note had no item written on it, and the output named an item anyway."}],"right":[{"id":"r1","text":"If a note names more than one item, then label each item separately and ask a person about any item left over."},{"id":"r2","text":"If the label does not match the item's own words, then flag that row for a person to re-check before it is posted."},{"id":"r3","text":"If the answer offers more than one label, then leave the row unlabelled and ask a person to decide."},{"id":"r4","text":"If the input has no item to label, then reply \"no item found\" and ask a person, instead of guessing."}],"answer":{"l1":"r1","l2":"r2","l3":"r3","l4":"r4"},"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}],"evidenceType":"product","feedback":{"correct":"Well matched - a fallback branch is only useful when it answers the exact failure the pressure test exposed.","incorrect":"Match each branch to what actually went wrong: a dropped item, a mislabelled item, an undecided label, and an invented item each need a different if-then."}}]},"formative":{"targets":"Checks that the plan survives a tricky input and gains a fallback branch","check":"Build/test log review: one normal run, one pressure test, one fallback branch, one logged change","successCriteria":"Prototype is run, a tricky input exposes a weak spot, and a fallback branch plus a logged reason are added.","evidence":"Completed build/test log with fallback branch."}},"g6-main-w8-core":{"id":"g6-main-w8-core","number":15,"week":8,"track":"main","type":"core","title":"Signature Solution - Building","summary":"Students build their signature solution by following their approved plan, evaluate and flag every output, and log one evidence-based improvement as evaluate -> change -> result.","duration":"45 minutes","strands":["CU","SD","CE","GE"],"objectives":["Build the solution by following the plan and prompt structure.","Evaluate outputs for accuracy and flag any that are incorrect or unclear.","Make at least one improvement based on an output check.","Keep personal and identifying data out of every prompt and keep a person in the loop for anything flagged."],"sections":[{"type":"text","body":["Today you build for real. You will follow your approved plan, run your inputs, and look hard at every output - not just the ones that look right. When you find an answer that is wrong or unclear, you flag it, change one thing, and watch what happens. This is how real systems get better: one careful change, one honest result. Keep every prompt free of personal data, and keep a person in the loop for anything you flag."],"role":"intro"},{"type":"list","heading":"I can…","items":["I can build my solution by following my approved plan.","I can evaluate outputs and flag ones that are wrong or unclear.","I can make one improvement and explain what changed and why.","I can keep personal data out of every prompt."],"role":"objectives"},{"type":"vocabulary","heading":"Words to know","terms":[{"term":"Build","definition":"Turning your approved plan into a working solution by following it step by step."},{"term":"Evaluate","definition":"Judging an output against your check to decide if it is correct and clear."},{"term":"Flag","definition":"Marking an output that is wrong or unclear so it gets attention and a fix."},{"term":"Improvement","definition":"One deliberate change made in response to an evaluation."},{"term":"Iteration loop","definition":"The evaluate -> change -> result cycle that makes a solution better over time."}],"role":"vocabulary"},{"type":"text","heading":"Follow the plan, do not improvise","body":["Building means running your approved prompt structure on defined inputs, not inventing new prompts on the spot.","Record each output before you change anything. That way you can see what your solution really does."],"role":"explain"},{"type":"text","heading":"Evaluate, flag, improve","body":["Evaluate each output against your check: correct, unclear, or wrong. Flag anything that fails.","Then make one improvement and re-run. Record it as **evaluate -> change -> result** so the cause of the improvement is clear."],"role":"explain"},{"type":"activity","label":"Let's try it - hands-on","body":["You will build your solution from your approved plan, evaluate the outputs, flag errors, and log one improvement. The teacher runs the AI on the board; you do not log in."],"items":["Restate your goal and check on your log.","Run your prompt structure on defined curated inputs and record each output.","Evaluate each output and flag the wrong or unclear ones.","Make one improvement in response to a flag and re-run it.","Log the loop as evaluate -> change -> result."],"role":"activity"},{"type":"list","heading":"Think about it","items":["How did you tell a subtly wrong output from a correct one?","Why is one change at a time better than many at once?"],"role":"discuss"},{"type":"list","heading":"My reflection","items":["Write the one improvement you made and why.","Write what you would evaluate more carefully next time."],"role":"reflect"}],"worksheet":{"title":"Week 8 - Core - Worksheet | Signature Solution - Building","questions":[{"id":"g6-main-w8-core-aq1","type":"ordering","marks":4,"prompt":"Maya is building her signature solution today. Put her five steps in the order the lesson says a build should follow.","items":[{"id":"i1","text":"Restate your goal and your check at the top of your log."},{"id":"i2","text":"Run your approved prompt structure on the curated inputs and record every output."},{"id":"i3","text":"Evaluate each output against your check and flag the ones that are wrong or unclear."},{"id":"i4","text":"Make one change in response to a flag and re-run it."},{"id":"i5","text":"Log what happened as evaluate -> change -> result."}],"answer":["i1","i2","i3","i4","i5"],"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}],"evidenceType":"product","feedback":{"correct":"Recording every output before you change anything is what lets you prove the change caused the improvement.","incorrect":"You evaluate and flag first, then change one thing: an improvement has to be a response to something you judged against your check."}},{"id":"g6-main-w8-core-aq2","type":"mcq","marks":2,"prompt":"Sami flags one wrong output. Before re-running, he adds examples to his prompt, rewrites the instruction and swaps the input - all at once. The new output is correct. What is the problem with Sami's improvement log?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"He cannot tell which of the three changes made the output correct."},{"id":"b","text":"He should have waited until all his inputs had been run before flagging anything."},{"id":"c","text":"He is not allowed to change his prompt once his plan has been approved."},{"id":"d","text":"An output that is correct after a change does not need to go in the log."}],"answer":"a","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}],"evidenceType":"product","feedback":{"correct":"With three changes in one re-run, the result cannot be traced to a cause, which is why the lesson asks for one change at a time.","incorrect":"The output did become correct, so the problem is not the flagging or the recording - it is that three changes at once hide which one actually worked."}},{"id":"g6-main-w8-core-aq3","type":"multi","marks":3,"prompt":"Layla says her build followed today's rules. Which of these show she built her solution the way the lesson describes? Select all that apply.","options":[{"id":"a","text":"She ran her approved prompt structure on the curated inputs she had planned."},{"id":"b","text":"She recorded every output before she changed anything."},{"id":"c","text":"She evaluated all five outputs, including the ones that looked right."},{"id":"d","text":"She invented a new prompt on the spot when the first output looked odd."},{"id":"e","text":"She used a classmate's full name as one of her test inputs."}],"answer":["a","b","c"],"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}],"evidenceType":"product","feedback":{"correct":"Following the approved structure, recording outputs before changing anything and checking even the outputs that look right are what make a build honest.","incorrect":"Inventing a new prompt on the spot is improvising, not building to the plan, and a classmate's name is personal data that must never go into a prompt."}},{"id":"g6-main-w8-core-aq4","type":"matching","marks":4,"prompt":"These four lines come from one student's improvement log. Match each line to the part of the loop it is doing.","left":[{"id":"L1","text":"Output 3 labelled a tablet charger as 'other', which fails my check."},{"id":"L2","text":"I marked output 3 so it would get attention and a fix."},{"id":"L3","text":"I added a rule to my prompt listing chargers and cables as devices."},{"id":"L4","text":"On the re-run, output 3 said 'devices' and 5 of 5 were correct."}],"right":[{"id":"R1","text":"Change"},{"id":"R2","text":"Result"},{"id":"R3","text":"Evaluate"},{"id":"R4","text":"Flag"}],"answer":{"L1":"R3","L2":"R4","L3":"R1","L4":"R2"},"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}],"evidenceType":"product","feedback":{"correct":"A complete loop shows the judgement, the flag it raised, the single change made in response, and the result that was actually observed.","incorrect":"Judging an output against your check is evaluating; marking it so it gets attention and a fix is flagging; what you did to the prompt is the change; what happened on the re-run is the result."}},{"id":"g6-main-w8-core-aq5","type":"truefalse","marks":1,"prompt":"True or false: once you have found one output that is clearly wrong, the outputs that already look right do not need to be evaluated.","answer":false,"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}],"evidenceType":"product","feedback":{"correct":"Every output is judged against the check, because a subtly wrong output is the one most likely to slip through looking fine.","incorrect":"Outputs that look right can still fail the check - that is why the lesson asks you to evaluate all of them, not just the obvious failures."}}]},"formative":{"targets":"Monitors build against the plan; each student logs one evaluation-based improvement","check":"Build check-in against plan + one logged improvement ('evaluate -> change -> result')","successCriteria":"Solution follows the plan, outputs are evaluated, and one improvement is made and explained.","evidence":"Build progress + improvement log."}},"g6-main-w8-lab":{"id":"g6-main-w8-lab","number":16,"week":8,"track":"main","type":"lab","title":"Extended Build and Evaluation (Lab)","summary":"Students complete their solution, run it across a six-input curated test set to report an accuracy count, handle a failure gracefully with their fallback, and finish with a three-question risk check.","duration":"45 minutes","strands":["CU","SD","CE","GE"],"objectives":["Complete a working solution and evaluate its outputs across several test inputs.","Handle an incorrect output gracefully and document the fix.","Run a basic risk check and confirm no personal data is used."],"sections":[{"type":"text","body":["This is the finish line for your signature solution - but finishing is not just getting one right answer. Today you prove it works by running it across several test inputs and counting how many it gets right. When one fails, your fallback catches it gracefully and you document the fix. Finally you run a basic risk check: is it accurate, is it clear, and is any prompt free of personal data? A solution you can measure and defend is one people can trust."],"role":"intro"},{"type":"list","heading":"I can…","items":["I can complete a working solution that follows my plan.","I can evaluate its outputs across several test inputs and measure accuracy.","I can handle an incorrect output gracefully using my fallback.","I can run a basic risk check and document the fix I made."],"role":"objectives"},{"type":"vocabulary","heading":"Words to know","terms":[{"term":"Test set","definition":"A small, curated group of inputs, including edge cases, used to measure how a solution performs."},{"term":"Accuracy count","definition":"The number of correct outputs out of the total tested, such as 5 out of 6."},{"term":"Graceful handling","definition":"Using the fallback so an incorrect output is caught safely, not presented as final."},{"term":"Documented fix","definition":"A written record of the change made to correct a failure and its effect."},{"term":"Risk check","definition":"A quick review of accuracy, clarity, and data safety before a solution is trusted."}],"role":"vocabulary"},{"type":"text","heading":"Prove it across several inputs","body":["One correct answer is not proof. Run your finished solution across a **test set** of several curated inputs and count how many outputs are correct.","Report accuracy as a simple count, like 5 out of 6, so your claim is honest and checkable."],"role":"explain"},{"type":"text","heading":"Handle failures and check the risks","body":["When an output is wrong, let your fallback catch it and keep a person in the loop, then document the fix and its effect.","Finish with a basic risk check: accurate enough? clear enough? any personal data used? The data answer must always be no, because we use only curated inputs."],"role":"explain"},{"type":"activity","label":"Let's try it - hands-on","body":["You will complete your solution, evaluate it across six curated inputs, handle any failure with your fallback, document the fix, and run a basic risk check. The teacher runs the AI on the board; you do not log in."],"items":["Confirm your build follows the plan and the fallback is wired in.","Define what counts as a correct output.","Run all six curated inputs and record each result.","Compute accuracy and handle any failure with your fallback, documenting the fix.","Complete the three-question risk check and note the biggest remaining risk."],"role":"activity"},{"type":"list","heading":"Think about it","items":["Why is an accuracy count across several inputs fairer than judging one output?","What is the biggest remaining risk in your solution, and who should check it?"],"role":"discuss"},{"type":"list","heading":"My reflection","items":["Write your accuracy result and the fix you documented.","Write one thing your risk check made you notice."],"role":"reflect"}],"worksheet":{"title":"Week 8 - Lab - Worksheet | Extended Build and Evaluation (Lab)","questions":[{"id":"g6-main-w8-lab-aq1","type":"numeric","marks":2,"prompt":"Omar runs his finished solution on all six curated test inputs. Inputs 1, 2, 4 and 6 give outputs that match his agreed definition of a correct output. Input 3 gives a wrong label. Input 5 gives an output so unclear that it fails his check. What number should Omar report as his accuracy count out of 6?","answer":4,"tolerance":0,"unit":"correct out of 6","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}],"evidenceType":"product","feedback":{"correct":"An output that is too unclear to pass the check counts as not correct, so Omar reports 4 out of 6.","incorrect":"Only outputs that meet the definition of correct are counted - the unclear one fails the check, so the honest count is 4 out of 6."}},{"id":"g6-main-w8-lab-aq2","type":"mcq","marks":2,"prompt":"Nadia's finished solution gives a wrong label for one of her test inputs. Which action counts as handling that failure gracefully, as the lesson describes it?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Her fallback catches the output and passes it to a person to check, and she records the fix she made and its effect."},{"id":"b","text":"She deletes the wrong output so her log only shows results she trusts."},{"id":"c","text":"She reports the output as final and adds a note saying AI is sometimes wrong."},{"id":"d","text":"She removes that input from her test set so her accuracy count goes up."}],"answer":"a","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}],"evidenceType":"product","feedback":{"correct":"Graceful handling means the fallback catches the wrong output, a person stays in the loop, and the fix and its effect are written down.","incorrect":"Deleting the output, presenting it as final or dropping the input all hide the failure - the fallback is there to catch it and pass it to a person."}},{"id":"g6-main-w8-lab-aq3","type":"multi","marks":3,"prompt":"Rashid tells the class 'my solution is accurate' after showing one input that came out right. According to the lesson, what is his claim missing? Select all that apply.","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A count across all six curated test inputs, such as 5 out of 6."},{"id":"b","text":"A definition of what counts as a correct output, agreed before testing."},{"id":"c","text":"Which of the six inputs failed, not just the total that passed."},{"id":"d","text":"A promise that his solution will never get an answer wrong."},{"id":"e","text":"The names of the classmates whose details he tested it on."}],"answer":["a","b","c"],"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}],"evidenceType":"product","feedback":{"correct":"An honest accuracy claim is a count across the whole curated test set, judged against a definition of correct agreed before testing, including the harder cases.","incorrect":"No solution can promise it will never be wrong, and personal data never belongs in a test set - what Rashid is missing is a checkable count across all his curated inputs."}},{"id":"g6-main-w8-lab-aq4","type":"matching","marks":3,"prompt":"Match each question in the three-question risk check to the evidence from a student's lab log that answers it.","left":[{"id":"L1","text":"Is it accurate enough?"},{"id":"L2","text":"Is it clear enough?"},{"id":"L3","text":"Was any personal data used?"}],"right":[{"id":"R1","text":"No - every input came from my curated test set, with no names or personal details."},{"id":"R2","text":"It matched my definition of a correct output on 5 of the 6 curated inputs."},{"id":"R3","text":"A reader can tell what each output means without asking me to explain it."}],"answer":{"L1":"R2","L2":"R3","L3":"R1"},"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}],"evidenceType":"product","feedback":{"correct":"Each risk-check question is answered with evidence: a count for accuracy, a reader test for clarity, and the curated test set for data safety.","incorrect":"Accuracy is answered by the count, clarity by whether a reader understands the output unaided, and the data question by the inputs being curated with no personal details."}},{"id":"g6-main-w8-lab-aq5","type":"truefalse","marks":1,"prompt":"True or false: because Layla's solution scored 6 out of 6 on her curated test set, she can skip the risk check.","answer":false,"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}],"evidenceType":"product","feedback":{"correct":"Accuracy is only one of the three risk-check questions - clarity and the personal-data question still have to be answered before a solution is trusted.","incorrect":"A perfect score on a small curated test set does not answer whether the outputs are clear or whether any personal data was used, so the risk check still has to be run."}}]},"formative":{"targets":"Confirms a completed solution is evaluated across several inputs, handles a failure, and passes a basic risk check","check":"Test-set evaluation log (accuracy count) + documented fix + completed risk check","successCriteria":"Solution is completed, evaluated across several inputs with an accuracy count, a failure is handled gracefully, and the risk check confirms no personal data.","evidence":"Completed test-set log, documented fix, and risk-check sheet."}},"g6-main-w9-core":{"id":"g6-main-w9-core","number":17,"week":9,"track":"main","type":"core","title":"Signature Solution - Refining & Finishing","summary":"Students judge their signature solution against accuracy, usefulness and risk, then make one improvement and prove with re-testing that it helped.","duration":"45 minutes","strands":["CU","SD","CE","GE"],"objectives":["Finish the solution so it meets the agreed success criteria.","Evaluate it for accuracy, usefulness and risk.","Explain one change that improved it."],"sections":[{"type":"text","heading":"Overview","body":["You are almost there. This week you become the judge of your own work, checking it for accuracy, usefulness and risk, then making one smart change that you can prove made it better."],"role":"intro"},{"type":"list","heading":"I can…","items":["I can finish my solution so it meets the success criteria our class agreed on.","I can judge my solution for accuracy, usefulness and risk using the rubric.","I can explain one change I made and say why it made the solution better."],"role":"objectives"},{"type":"vocabulary","heading":"Words to know","terms":[{"term":"Refine","definition":"To improve something on purpose by comparing it to your goals."},{"term":"Accuracy","definition":"How often the system gives the right or sensible answer."},{"term":"Usefulness","definition":"How much the solution really helps the user do their task."},{"term":"Risk","definition":"What could go wrong or who could be harmed, including privacy."},{"term":"Evidence","definition":"Proof from testing, not just an opinion."}],"role":"vocabulary"},{"type":"text","heading":"Judging your solution three ways","body":["A good builder does not just ask 'do I like it?'. You measure your work against the criteria the class agreed.","**Accuracy**: test with examples where you know the right answer. **Usefulness**: check it helps your real user. **Risk**: ask what could go wrong and whether any private data is exposed."],"role":"explain"},{"authored":true,"type":"reference","label":"Criteria card","heading":"The three lenses","intro":"Judge your solution through each lens in turn. A solution can be accurate and still fail the other two.","entries":[{"term":"Accuracy","asks":"How often does it give the right or sensible answer?","test":"Test it with examples where you already know the correct answer, and record the score."},{"term":"Usefulness","asks":"Does it actually help your user do their task?","test":"Check it against the real task you designed it for, not a task that happens to suit it."},{"term":"Risk","asks":"What could go wrong, and who could be harmed?","test":"Check for unfair results and for any private data being exposed."}],"role":"reference"},{"type":"text","heading":"One change, proven","body":["You do not need many changes. Choose the one improvement that matters most to your user.","Make the change, then re-test. If accuracy went up and nothing else broke, you have evidence it worked. Write it in your refinement note."],"role":"explain"},{"authored":true,"type":"diagram","variant":"loop","label":"How to prove a change worked","heading":"One change at a time, with evidence","altText":"A loop: judge the solution against the criteria, change one thing, run it again, and judge again to see whether that change helped.","steps":[{"label":"Judge it","detail":"against your criteria"},{"label":"Change one thing","detail":"and write down which"},{"label":"Run it again","detail":"on the same input"},{"label":"Judge again","detail":"did that change help?","accent":true}],"repeatLabel":"still short of the criteria — go round again","caption":"Same input each time, one change each time. **Change two things at once and you cannot say which one worked** — which is the evidence you are being asked for.","role":"diagram"},{"type":"activity","label":"Let's try it - hands-on","heading":"Let's try it - hands-on","body":["Work with your partner. Judge your solution against the rubric, choose one improvement, make it, and prove it helped."],"items":["Read the three lenses on the criteria card.","Run one accuracy test with known answers and record the score.","Check usefulness against your real user's task.","Do a basic risk check for harm, fairness and privacy.","Pick one improvement, make it, and re-test.","Swap for 'two stars and a wish' feedback."],"role":"activity"},{"type":"list","heading":"Think about it","items":["Which lens was hardest to judge, and why?","How did re-testing change your confidence in your solution?"],"role":"discuss"},{"type":"list","heading":"My reflection","items":["Write the one change you made and the evidence it helped.","Name one risk you are now sure your solution avoids."],"role":"reflect"}],"worksheet":{"title":"Week 9 - Core - Worksheet | Signature Solution - Refining & Finishing","questions":[{"id":"g6-main-w9-core-aq1","type":"mcq","marks":2,"prompt":"Maryam's team built a homework-reminder helper for their class teacher. It gave the right reminder on all 10 test cases where the team already knew the correct answer, but the teacher had to type every pupil's timetable in again each morning, so she stopped using it. Judging the solution through the three lenses, which statement is right?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"It passes accuracy but fails usefulness, because it does not help the teacher do her real task."},{"id":"b","text":"It fails accuracy, because a solution the teacher stops using cannot be giving right answers."},{"id":"c","text":"It passes all three lenses, because it got every test case right."},{"id":"d","text":"It passes usefulness because the reminders were correct, but fails accuracy because of the extra typing."}],"answer":"a","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}],"evidenceType":"product","feedback":{"correct":"Correct - a solution can pass accuracy and still fail usefulness, because usefulness is judged against the real task the user has to do.","incorrect":"Accuracy is about how often the answers are right, and usefulness is about whether the solution helps the user do their real task - here the answers were right but the task got harder."}},{"id":"g6-main-w9-core-aq2","type":"matching","marks":3,"prompt":"Match each lens to the test that gives you evidence for it.","left":[{"id":"l1","text":"Accuracy"},{"id":"l2","text":"Usefulness"},{"id":"l3","text":"Risk"}],"right":[{"id":"r1","text":"Run it on ten examples where you already know the right answer, and record the score"},{"id":"r2","text":"Watch your real user do the actual task they need, using your solution"},{"id":"r3","text":"Check whether any results are unfair, and whether private data could be seen"}],"answer":{"l1":"r1","l2":"r2","l3":"r3"},"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}],"evidenceType":"product","feedback":{"correct":"Correct - each lens has its own test, so a judgement only counts when it comes from the matching kind of evidence.","incorrect":"Check the criteria card: accuracy is tested with known answers, usefulness against the user's real task, and risk by looking for unfair results and exposed private data."}},{"id":"g6-main-w9-core-aq3","type":"ordering","marks":4,"prompt":"Put these steps in the order the lesson says to refine a solution and prove the change helped.","items":[{"id":"s1","text":"Judge the solution against accuracy, usefulness and risk"},{"id":"s2","text":"Choose the one improvement that matters most to your user"},{"id":"s3","text":"Make that change to the solution"},{"id":"s4","text":"Re-test the same known-answer cases and compare with the earlier score"},{"id":"s5","text":"Write the refinement note saying what changed and what the evidence shows"}],"answer":["s1","s2","s3","s4","s5"],"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}],"evidenceType":"product","feedback":{"correct":"Correct - you judge first so the change is chosen for a reason, and you re-test afterwards so you have evidence rather than a feeling.","incorrect":"The order matters: judge against the lenses, choose the one improvement your user needs most, make it, re-test the same cases, then record the evidence."}},{"id":"g6-main-w9-core-aq4","type":"truefalse","marks":1,"prompt":"Omar re-runs his ten known-answer cases after making a change, and his score rises from 6 out of 10 to 9 out of 10. He writes in his refinement note that the change worked. He compared only the two totals and never looked at which cases changed, so he does not know whether a case that used to pass now fails. Is that enough evidence?","answer":false,"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}],"evidenceType":"product","feedback":{"correct":"Correct - the lesson asks for accuracy up AND nothing else broken. Two totals cannot show that: 6 to 9 is the same arithmetic whether three cases improved, or four improved while one that used to pass now fails.","incorrect":"A higher total is not proof on its own. 6 to 9 looks the same whether three cases improved or four improved and one broke, so you have to compare case by case."}},{"id":"g6-main-w9-core-aq5","type":"multi","marks":3,"prompt":"A team says their change made the solution better. Which of these count as evidence for that claim, rather than opinion? Choose all that apply.","options":[{"id":"o1","text":"The score on the same ten known-answer tests went from 6 out of 10 to 9 out of 10"},{"id":"o2","text":"Their partner said the new version looks much nicer"},{"id":"o3","text":"Re-running the cases that already worked showed that all of them still pass"},{"id":"o4","text":"The team felt more confident once the change was made"},{"id":"o5","text":"Their real user finished the task the solution was designed for, which they could not do before"}],"answer":["o1","o3","o5"],"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}],"evidenceType":"product","feedback":{"correct":"Correct - evidence comes from testing that someone else could repeat, not from how the team feels about the change.","incorrect":"Evidence has to come from testing: scores on known-answer cases, re-running the cases that already passed, or the real user managing the task. How something looks or feels is an opinion."}}]},"formative":{"targets":"Self- and peer-evaluation against the tiered rubric (Emerging = expected)","check":"Structured self/peer review using the rubric; one improvement acted upon","successCriteria":"Meets the Emerging criteria; names an accuracy/usefulness/risk judgement and one improvement.","evidence":"Completed self/peer rubric + refinement note."}}}};
}

